# TRELLIS.2 image workflow

## Which image is which (read this first)

Two files per lump share a name and have different jobs — mixing them up has
already cost a round of confusion (2026-08-15):

| File | Style | Consumer |
|------|-------|----------|
| `<LUMP>-depth-shapehint.png` | display-grade pixel art, 2D | `DisplayRedrawAllowlist` — the Enhanced + 3D **Off** redraw |
| `<LUMP>-depth-shapehint-v2.png` | volumetric render | TRELLIS.2 conditioning, and the texture the mesh is painted with (`project_hint_texture.py`) |
| `<LUMP>-depth-shapehint-45deg.png` | alternative conditioning | historical; the Space consumes one image |

Since the monster track (2026-08-13) **v2 is the default conditioning**, not an
escalation: monsters reconstruct flat from pixel-art hints. Monster frames
therefore need v2 ONLY — monsters are excluded from the display-redraw
allowlist, because that gate is rotation-aware (all eight rotation lumps per
frame), so a lone v1 for one frame routes nowhere.

## Native inputs

- Decode the original WAD sprite with the first `PLAYPAL`; do not use EdgeMix or Super-xBR.
- Prefer the front-facing `A1` frame. Reuse one image when variants share a sprite (for example SARG demon/spectre).
- Crop only fully transparent padding, upscale by the largest integer nearest-neighbor factor that keeps the subject at most 416 px, then center it on a 512×512 RGBA canvas.
- Save as `Textures/Trellis2/NativeInputs/<LUMP>-trellis.png`.

## Front depth shape-hints

- Use the native input as the reference image and generate one isolated item per square image.
- Preserve identity: silhouette, pose, proportions, palette, symbols, equipment, limb count, and recognizable asymmetry.
- Add only useful 3D cues: rounded/cylindrical forms, side thickness, layered surfaces, limb separation, attachment points, and controlled highlights/shadows.
- Keep crisp chunky pixel art, a hard readable contour, centered framing, and a transparent/checkerboard background. No floor, scenery, cast shadow, text, or invented rear design.
- Save as `Textures/Trellis2/ShapeHints/<LUMP>-depth-shapehint.png`; never overwrite the native input.

## 45-degree variants

- Reference the accepted front depth shape-hint, not the low-resolution native input.
- Rotate about 45° around the vertical axis into a consistent front-right three-quarter view; expose side depth and overlap without becoming a full profile.
- Keep the same design, pose, proportions, palette, equipment, and number/arrangement of limbs.
- Save separately as `Textures/Trellis2/ShapeHints/<LUMP>-depth-shapehint-45deg.png`.
- Treat it as an alternative conditioning input: the standard TRELLIS.2 image workflow consumes one image, not front and 45° simultaneously.

## v2 escalation for planar results

- A nominal 45° redraw is not sufficient when roots, branches, limbs, or foliage still occupy one projected plane.
- Keep the overall presentation frontal and make topology explicit: radial roots, surfaces and branches around the full circumference, strong foreshortening, front/rear overlap, visible attachment points, and coherent contact shadows.
- For leafy assets, split flat crowns into separated boughs at multiple depths: larger/brighter toward-camera masses, darker/smaller rear masses, edge-on clusters, and gaps exposing branch structure.
- Prefer natural volumetric shading and readable geometry over strict pixel-art treatment. Preserve palette, major landmarks, and identity, but allow the outer silhouette to change when the original silhouette forces a card-like reconstruction.
- Save as `Textures/Trellis2/ShapeHints/<LUMP>-depth-shapehint-v2.png`.
- Judge the generated TRELLIS mesh, not only the conditioning image. Geometry success with substantial identity drift is `CONDITIONAL SUCCESS` until an in-game Enhanced gate confirms scale, silhouette, palette, and environmental style.

Inspect every generated result for identity drift or invented anatomy before presenting it. Ambiguous single-view sprites require an interactive user gate.
